claude: 
    This one's squarely in your wheelhouse — it overlaps a lot with the Tao ICM material you've been working from, though it's a more essayistic take. Moradifam's core claim: as routine mathematical production gets automated, the distinctly human parts (problem framing, judgment, taste) become more visible and valuable, not less. He walks through education (cognitive offloading, illusion of explanatory depth, the assessment crisis), research (the Davies/AlphaTensor/FunSearch lineage plus the 2026 Erdős results), and then the epistemology — the shift from constructive to hermeneutic understanding, where mathematicians become interpreters of opaque machine output.

    The section most relevant to your attention-markets/canonization angle is probably the third one, where he touches "what the mathematical community should regard as interesting, deep, or worth understanding" — but notably he treats that judgment as stably human without asking how those collective judgments form or whether AI changes the canonization dynamics themselves. That's essentially the gap your extension fills.

    What did you want to do with it — critique, compare against your draft, mine it for citations, something else?

