    Also, there is this part on the intersection with Tao that even today some people are influencers of the field, but we do have this naive and blind faith in them that they want the good of the community and science, but that's not a very strong assumption. The influencer economy might make it better or worse. In most cases (all cases?) so far it has made things worse imo.

claude:
    Examined credibility assumptions and incentive misalignments in academic influence

    You're poking at something the discipline mostly refuses to examine. The trust structure in mathematics works on a kind of implicit clergy model: figures like Tao, Gowers, Terence-tier influencers get to declare what's interesting, and the community treats their taste as disinterested — as if canonization decisions flow from pure mathematical judgment rather than from people with careers, incentives, aesthetic biases, and finite attention. The assumption isn't that they're corrupt; it's subtler — it's that their attention allocation is treated as a public good when it's actually a private act with public consequences. A Tao blog post about a problem can redirect years of collective effort. Nobody audits whether that redirection was optimal; the prestige itself becomes the justification.

    Historically the assumption sort of worked, but arguably for structural reasons that are eroding, not because mathematicians are unusually virtuous. The old gatekeepers operated in a low-velocity, low-visibility attention economy: journals, seminars, letters. Feedback loops were slow, audiences were small, and there was little to gain from attention-maximizing behavior because attention didn't convert to much. The influencer economy changes the conversion rate. When visibility translates into grants, positions, media presence, and now AI-training-data prominence — which papers and problems the models learn to consider central — the incentive to be seen judging well starts competing with judging well. Your metric-optimization frame applies directly: the moment "influence" becomes measurable and monetizable, it gets optimized for, and the optimization target diverges from the thing it proxied.

    On "all cases so far it has made things worse" — I'd push back to maybe 90%. There are real counterexamples: Polymath-style open collaboration, working mathematicians blogging proofs-in-progress, arXiv culture generally — these expanded who could participate in judgment rather than concentrating it. The failure mode isn't visibility itself; it's when the selection mechanism for who gets amplified shifts from "trusted by peers over decades" to "performs well on engagement metrics." Math has been partially protected because its audience is small and expert — engagement farming doesn't work well when your readers can check your proofs. The danger with AI is that it changes the audience: if models become the primary readers and rankers of mathematics, then whatever biases exist in the current canon get frozen in and amplified, and the influencers of today become the training distribution of tomorrow. That's a stronger version of your path-dependence argument than anything in Moradifam's paper — he assumes the "collective culture of the field" is a stable arbiter, but the collective culture is precisely what's up for grabs.

