    It is the case when someone agrees on some topic with me I'm not immediately buying their idea, I ask why, and there have been a plenty of time that their reasoning was very different from mine. And that difference is sometimes helping me gain new perspective and sometimes it makes me disregard their conclusion. Here the decision is not the end goal as some might portrait, the process to come to that conclusion is

claude:
    Recognized epistemological rigor matters more than mere agreement

    That habit — interrogating agreement as skeptically as disagreement — is rarer than it should be, and it cuts against how most people treat consensus. The default social epistemology is conclusion-matching: you said X, I believe X, therefore we're allies and your belief strengthens mine. But two people at the same conclusion via different reasoning aren't actually in the same epistemic state, and the interesting information is entirely in the delta. If their path is sound and different from yours, you now have two independent lines of support — genuine corroboration. If their path is broken, their agreement is evidence against your confidence in a weird way, because it shows the conclusion is reachable by bad reasoning, which means your own arrival there needs re-checking. Agreement without shared reasoning is coincidence wearing the costume of confirmation.

    This is also exactly the mechanism that breaks in the influencer dynamics from before. Deference to a Tao-tier figure is conclusion-adoption with the reasoning step amputated — you inherit the belief but not the justification, so you can't detect when the justification wouldn't survive your own scrutiny. Scale that up and you get a community whose beliefs are correlated not because the reasoning independently converged but because everyone copied from the same node. It looks like consensus, which we treat as strong evidence, but the effective sample size is one. That's the epistemically hollow version of agreement, and it's precisely what the attention economy manufactures: it propagates conclusions at high velocity while reasoning, which is expensive to transmit and verify, gets stripped in transit.

    And it loops back to the AI-outsourcing thread cleanly: an AI answer is the limit case of conclusion-without-process. When you ask a person "why," the answer sometimes reveals their reasoning was different from yours — that's the perspective-gaining moment you're describing. The fluent AI explanation is engineered to feel like that moment without necessarily being one, which is Moradifam's illusion-of-explanatory-depth point but sharpened: the danger isn't just students confusing exposure with ownership, it's everyone losing the practice of treating "why" as the real question and the conclusion as merely its byproduct.